        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int 2 x e^{2 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=2 x^{2} + 1$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(4 x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int 2 x e^{2 x^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=e^{u}+C.\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\int e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}\\left(e^{u}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\left(e^{2 x^{2} + 1}\\right)+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
