      "question": {
        ":question/id": "d9ce85b4-aa4d-55cf-a317-e01cee39804d",
        ":question/problem": "Find an antiderivative.\n\n$$\n\\int x^{2} e^{1 - x^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n\n{{answer}} $+C$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=1 - x^{3}$. Then $\\mathrm{d}u=(- 3 x^{2})\\,\\mathrm{d}x$.\n\nIncluding the differential factor gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}&\\int x^{2} e^{1 - x^{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\\\&=- \\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the integration rule:\n\n$$\n\\int e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u=e^{u}+C.\n$$\n\n$$\n- \\frac{1}{3}\\left(\\int e^{u}\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\right)=- \\frac{1}{3}\\left(e^{u}\\right)+C.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back:\n\n$$\n- \\frac{1}{3}\\left(e^{1 - x^{3}}\\right)+C.\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "c3f28dcb-f3fe-51c5-a112-14c002c8210c",
